      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "VRChat does not allow users under the age of thirteen to be on the platform (see Section 2 of VRChat's Terms of Service [https://hello.vrchat.com/legal]). I agree that the behavior of minors is an issue for the Quest platform of VRChat, and having parents' permission to sign up and use VRChat should be enforced.\nI also agree that there should be some sort of way to tell is a user is a minor between 13-17, but the issue is that anyone can fake their age; we've all done it before. It's good to use mindful judgement of someone's behavior and mannerisms to see if they are acting mature.\nIf a user is under the age of thirteen, it should be reported to VRChat, but it's not something enforced as a priority. That's why there is a Canny thread for this already.\nhttps://feedback.vrchat.com/feature-requests/p/add-underaged-reporting\nWhen someone uploads content to VRChat, they can check boxes to tag if there is NSFW, Gore/Blood, and other filters. Since Steam and Oculus accounts are not official VRChat accounts, I have a feeling that it would be a good idea to automatically filter out adult content for both avatars and worlds, for all of those accounts until they log in with a VRChat account and have a confirmed age of eighteen or over.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2021-07-17T06:35:40.618Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I’ve noticed an extreme increase of kids under the age of 13 joining and being on vrchat in the last year. This is concerning cause situations may or do occur and the environment isnt safe for the kids. As well as there isn’t anything that can be done when players realize there’s a kid. I’m talking about there being 6 to 10 year olds. I think vrchat should have a way for users to notify when an account is underage as well as put other measures. Maybe once people notify an account is under the age limit there can be a process for them to confirm their age. Or provide them their own spaces.",
